  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2})=2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)) \\\\  & =2(2t)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =4t\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+6 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,({e}^{-3/2}) & ={e}^{-3/2}(4ln\\,({e}^{-3/2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(-6+2) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t={e}^{-3/2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4{e}^{-3/2}.$",
